{
  "paraId": "643806D4",
  "style": "",
  "text": "More significantly, our study explores the impact and potential mechanism of low-dose BIBR1532 combined with radiotherapy on the TIME. Co-administration of BIBR1532 with RT activates the cGAS/STING signalling pathway, although BIBR1532 alone does not elicit this response. This synergy with IR augments cGAS sensitivity to cytoplasmic dsDNA, possibly because BIBR1532 affects telomerase activity. Telomere shortening to a critical length requires a substantial time lag [38], whereas ionizing radiation (IR) directly induces exogenous DNA damage, causing chromosomal breaks and substantial DSBs. The non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) pathway then rapidly repairs these DSBs, but this template-free mechanism may generate structurally aberrant chromosomal fragments that are improperly segregated and culminate in micronuclei [39, 40]. Micronuclei can rupture and release dsDNA into the cytoplasm, activating cGAS, which catalyses synthesis of cyclic GMP-AMP (cGAMP) and triggers the innate immune response [41]. Notably, BIBR1532 intensifies IR-induced DNA damage, enhancing phosphorylation of STING, TBK1, and IRF3 in both tumour cells and dendritic cells (DCs), which increases interferon-beta (IFN-β) expression, fostering DC maturation and inciting an anti-tumour immune response."
}

